WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:06.880
Marius still lived in the old Gorbeau house, but he paid no attention to anyone living there.

2
00:00:06.881 --> 00:00:14.108
The only other tenants left in the building besides himself were the Jondrettes, the miserable family whose rent he had paid.

3
00:00:14.109 --> 00:00:17.785
It was now February of 1832.

4
00:00:17.786 --> 00:00:24.959
Marius went out for dinner one night as it was growing dark. He was lost in his own thoughts as he walked along.

5
00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:32.935
Suddenly he felt someone elbow him in the side. He looked up and saw two girls in rags running past him.

6
00:00:32.936 --> 00:00:41.189
They were breathless, frightened, and obviously in flight. Apparently they hadn’t seen him and had jostled him in passing.

7
00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:49.332
Marius could see their tangled hair, tattered skirts, and bare feet. They were talking to each other as they ran.

8
00:00:49.333 --> 00:00:56.007
"The cops came," said the taller one. "I thought they were going to arrest me!"

9
00:00:56.008 --> 00:01:01.578
"I saw them and ran as fast as I could," replied the other girl.

10
00:01:01.579 --> 00:01:09.078
They darted under the trees of the boulevard. Their clothing made a pale glow in the darkness, which soon faded away.

11
00:01:09.079 --> 00:01:17.237
Marius stopped to watch them for a moment. He was about to start walking again when he spotted a small gray packet on the ground.

12
00:01:17.238 --> 00:01:22.721
He bent over and picked it up. It was an envelope containing some papers.

13
00:01:22.722 --> 00:01:28.562
"Those poor girls must have dropped this." He called after them, but they had already disappeared.

14
00:01:28.563 --> 00:01:33.895
That evening when he was back in his room, he took out the envelope that he had found.

15
00:01:33.896 --> 00:01:41.149
Perhaps if he opened it, he would find an address for one of the girls. The envelope contained four letters.

16
00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:47.483
These letters were addressed to different people, but in each one, the writer was begging for money.

17
00:01:47.484 --> 00:01:59.205
Two letters were addressed to noblewomen, another to a businessman, and the last to "the generous man" who attends mass at the Church of Saint Jacques du Haut Pas.

18
00:01:59.206 --> 00:02:11.610
The letters seemed to come from four different individuals: a Spanish cavalry officer; an unfortunate mother of six children; a poet; and P. Fabantou, an actor.

19
00:02:11.611 --> 00:02:17.681
But strangely enough, they were all written in the same handwriting on the same kind of coarse yellow paper.

20
00:02:17.682 --> 00:02:20.948
And they all smelled of the same wretched tobacco.

21
00:02:20.949 --> 00:02:26.999
Some effort had been made to change the writing style by varying the length of words and sentences.

22
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:34.578
But the same spelling errors occurred across all four letters. There was only one thing that Marius could conclude.

23
00:02:34.579 --> 00:02:42.737
"They must have all been written by the same person," he said to himself. "But why? And how were those girls involved?"

24
00:02:42.738 --> 00:02:50.578
It was too great a puzzle for Marius. He returned the letters to the envelope, tossed it in a corner, and went to bed.

25
00:02:50.579 --> 00:02:57.157
At seven o’clock the next morning, Marius had just started working when there was a tap at his door.

26
00:02:57.158 --> 00:03:01.736
Marius owned nothing of value, so he hardly ever locked his door.

27
00:03:01.737 --> 00:03:06.974
In fact, when he went out, he left the key in the lock on the outside.

28
00:03:06.975 --> 00:03:13.895
The housekeeper had warned him that he would be robbed someday, and indeed someone had stolen an old pair of boots.

29
00:03:13.896 --> 00:03:17.974
But that loss was not enough to make Marius change his habits.

30
00:03:17.975 --> 00:03:20.670
There was another gentle rap at the door.

31
00:03:20.671 --> 00:03:24.553
"Come in," said Marius, thinking it was the housekeeper.

32
00:03:24.554 --> 00:03:29.712
"What do you want?" he asked without raising his eyes from the books and papers on his table.

33
00:03:29.713 --> 00:03:38.696
A voice replied, "I beg your pardon, monsieur . . ." It was a rasping voice like that of a congested old man.

34
00:03:38.697 --> 00:03:43.863
Marius turned quickly and saw a girl standing in the half-open door.

35
00:03:43.864 --> 00:03:51.767
She was a pale, thin creature dressed only in a ragged blouse and skirt with a string for a belt.

36
00:03:51.768 --> 00:03:58.093
Marius stared in astonishment at her dirty shoulders, red hands, and bare feet.

37
00:03:58.094 --> 00:04:04.768
Perhaps she had once been pretty, but now she was missing some teeth and her eyes were dull.

38
00:04:04.769 --> 00:04:07.097
"What do you want?" he asked.

39
00:04:07.098 --> 00:04:10.934
"Here’s a letter for you, Monsieur Marius."

40
00:04:10.935 --> 00:04:15.498
Without waiting for him to invite her in, the girl entered the room.

41
00:04:15.499 --> 00:04:21.202
Large holes in her skirt revealed her long limbs and sharp knees. She was shivering.

42
00:04:21.203 --> 00:04:23.831
Marius began to read the letter.

43
00:04:23.832 --> 00:04:26.246
My dear neighbor:

44
00:04:26.247 --> 00:04:33.998
I have learned of your kindness toward me, that you paid my rent last summer. Bless you, young man.

45
00:04:33.999 --> 00:04:41.490
My daughter will tell you that we have been without a morsel of bread for two days. There are four of us and my spouse is sick.

46
00:04:41.491 --> 00:04:48.718
I hope that I may rely on your generous heart to lavish upon me some small gift.

47
00:04:48.719 --> 00:04:51.308
Jondrette

48
00:04:51.309 --> 00:04:56.887
Marius immediately recognized that this letter came from the same source as the other four.

49
00:04:56.888 --> 00:05:01.793
There was the identical handwriting on the same paper that smelled of tobacco.

50
00:05:01.794 --> 00:05:06.204
It must have been the Jondrette girls whom he encountered the previous night!

51
00:05:06.205 --> 00:05:10.038
Meanwhile the girl was pacing Marius’ room.

52
00:05:10.039 --> 00:05:17.609
She moved the chairs; she examined his things on the bureau; she patted his clothes and searched the corners of the room.

53
00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:22.609
"Oh, you have a mirror!" she said, preening in front of it.

54
00:05:22.610 --> 00:05:25.438
All the while she hummed to herself.

55
00:05:25.439 --> 00:05:31.688
Nothing was more sorrowful than to see her amusing herself by flitting around the room like a bird.

56
00:05:31.689 --> 00:05:36.587
With the right upbringing, this girl might have been someone sweet and charming.

57
00:05:36.588 --> 00:05:39.934
Marius watched her as she moved toward his table.

58
00:05:39.935 --> 00:05:44.839
"Ah, books!" Her eyes brightened.

59
00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:49.180
"I can read." She picked up an open book and read aloud.

60
00:05:49.181 --> 00:06:00.172
"‘General Bauduin received the order to take five battalions to the chateau in the middle of the plain of Waterloo.’"

61
00:06:00.173 --> 00:06:07.410
She stopped. "I know Waterloo. It was a battle against the English. My father was there."

62
00:06:07.411 --> 00:06:13.085
The girl put down the book and took up a pen. "I can write too! Would you like to see?"

63
00:06:13.086 --> 00:06:17.243
She dipped the pen in ink and wrote, "The cops are here."

64
00:06:17.244 --> 00:06:23.243
Throwing down the pen, she said, "Look at this! There are no mistakes in spelling.

65
00:06:23.244 --> 00:06:28.719
My sister and I received an education. We haven’t always been like this."

66
00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:33.845
Then she gazed at Marius. "You’re a very handsome boy, monsieur."

67
00:06:33.846 --> 00:06:36.973
Marius blushed and turned away from her.

68
00:06:36.974 --> 00:06:40.235
"I have an envelope here that I think belongs to you."

69
00:06:40.236 --> 00:06:43.220
He handed her the letters and she clapped her hands.

70
00:06:43.221 --> 00:06:49.949
"Oh, thank you! My sister and I had to lie and tell our father that we had delivered these.

71
00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:56.878
Now I’ll take this one to the old generous fellow who goes to mass, and perhaps he’ll give us something for our breakfast.

72
00:06:56.879 --> 00:06:59.203
We haven’t eaten for days."

73
00:06:59.204 --> 00:07:08.266
That reminded Marius of her father’s note. He felt in his waistcoat pocket and came up with five francs, which he handed to the girl.

74
00:07:08.267 --> 00:07:13.346
"Thank you, monsieur, and now I’m off to find the old man."

75
00:07:13.347 --> 00:07:21.164
On her way out, she spotted a moldy crust of bread in the dust on the bureau. She pounced on it and ate it.

